<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm at the Market. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at the Market.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs. Reading: Follow written instructions for making or doing something simple. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify the main idea in simple paragraphs about locations (e.g., "I'm at school."). Can follow written instructions to identify or describe landmarks in a town (e.g., "Find the library on the map." "Point to the park in the picture."). Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable and two-syllable familiar words (e.g., blending the sounds /ɪ-n/ to form the word "in" and the sounds /b-ɪ-g/ to form the word "big").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or large pictures of familiar places introduced in Lesson 1 (e.g., market, school, park, library, hospital, home). Students will orally recall the names of the places. Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard, points to the written word, and asks, "Where am I?" Students read the word aloud (e.g., "market"). The teacher then says, "I'm at the market," emphasizing the contraction and preposition. This activates prior vocabulary and introduces the reading focus.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes simple sentences on the board or chart paper, such as "I'm at the school." or "I'm at the park." The teacher models reading each sentence clearly, pointing to each word as it is read. The teacher specifically highlights and pronounces the /sk/ sound in "school" and the /m/ sound in "market" when those words appear, then asks students to repeat. The teacher demonstrates blending phonemes by slowly saying sounds like /p/-/a/-/r/-/k/ and then quickly blending them to "park," showing the written word and having students read it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions to check understanding of the sentences.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "I'm at the school." "Am I at the park?" (No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "I'm at the market." "What place is this?" (Market)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue or two simple paragraphs titled "Where Am I Today?" The text will feature a child describing their day visiting various places. For example:
                    <br>
                    <i>Hello! I'm Leo. Today, I'm busy. First, I'm at the market. I see many fruits. It is big. Next, I walk to the library. I read a book. The library is quiet. Then, I go to the park. I play with my friends. The park is fun! Finally, I'm at home. I am tired.</i>
                    <br>
                    The text will include contractions ("I'm"), prepositions of place ("at," "in"), and target vocabulary (market, library, park, home, busy, quiet, big, walk, read, play, see).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs. Reading: Can identify the main idea in simple paragraphs about locations (e.g., "I'm at school.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud once. Students listen and follow along in their books/worksheets. Then, the teacher asks: "Where is Leo today?" (Expected answer: market, library, park, home – or "many places").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read the text independently. The teacher provides simple True/False statements related to the text, for example:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo is at the market first." (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"He reads a book at the hospital." (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The park is quiet." (False)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple paragraphs, following written instructions, identifying main ideas, using contractions (I'm), prepositions of place (at, in, near), present simple, and vocabulary (market, school, park, library, store, hospital, bakery, restaurant, busy, quiet, big, small, walk, read, buy, go, see). Phonemic awareness for /sk/, /m/, and blending simple CVC/CVCC words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Sentences to Pictures (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with 4-5 short, descriptive sentences about places on one side and corresponding pictures of the places on the other. Students read each sentence and draw a line to match it to the correct picture. For example:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I'm at the bakery. I buy bread." (Picture of a bakery)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I'm at the hospital. I see a nurse." (Picture of a hospital)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The school is big. I study here." (Picture of a school)</li>
                            </ul>
                            The teacher guides students to read sentences aloud in small groups, emphasizing /sk/ in school and /m/ in market.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Fill-in-the-Blank with Prepositions/Contractions (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with short sentences describing locations, but with blanks for prepositions ("at," "in") or contractions ("I'm"). Students read the sentences and choose the correct word from a word bank to complete them.
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"___ the park. I play here." (I'm at)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My home is small. I am ___ home." (at)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The market is ___ town." (in)</li>
                            </ul>
                            The teacher walks around, listening to students read quietly and providing support for word blending (e.g., blending /ɪ-n/ to form "in").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Read and Point" (Pair Work / Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a simple map of a town with labeled places (market, park, library, store, home). Partner A reads a short instruction or description aloud to Partner B. Partner B listens, then points to the correct place on the map. They then switch roles.
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner A: "Point to the market. It's busy."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner B: "Find the library. It is near the park."</li>
                            </ul>
                            This activity practices reading instructions and identifying places, with peer correction.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Town Map" Reading Scavenger Hunt.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a larger, simple map of a town with various places (market, school, park, library, store, bakery, restaurant, hospital, home) clearly labeled or represented by simple icons. They also receive a list of 4-5 written "clues" or instructions. Each clue is a simple paragraph describing a place or an action related to it. The goal is for the group to read all the clues and correctly identify/locate the places on their map by circling or marking them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Clues:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This place is busy. I go here to buy food. I'm at the market." (Students find and circle 'Market'.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like to play here with my friends. It is near the school." (Students find and circle 'Park'.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I am quiet here. I can read many books. It is the library." (Students find and circle 'Library'.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My home is small and clean. I am at home now." (Students find and circle 'Home'.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Groups must read and discuss the clues to agree on the correct location. The teacher observes, listening for students reading aloud and discussing the meaning of the paragraphs, and supporting them with blending phonemes if needed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Quick Check worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students individually complete a "Read and Draw" worksheet. They receive 3-4 short, simple sentences describing a place and an activity (e.g., "I'm at the school. I study English."), and below each sentence, there is a box for drawing. Students read each sentence and draw the described place and activity. This assesses their ability to read and outline the meaning of simple paragraphs and follow instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Accurately identifying the main idea of the paragraph (drawing the correct place).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Instruction Following:</b> Drawing the described activity correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Implicit understanding of target nouns and verbs (market, school, park, buy, read, play).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> (Assessed during production stage) Blending sounds for familiar words like "big" or "in" when reading aloud.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short prompts:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about these places: __________ (e.g., market, school)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reading instructions for maps is (easy/medium/hard)."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word you read today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the easiest part of reading about places?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully identified the main idea in simple paragraphs and followed written instructions for map tasks. The phonemic awareness blending was supported well during modeling and practice. Some students still struggled with specific sight words, which will need more repetition. The group scavenger hunt promoted collaborative reading effectively. For the next lesson, I will reinforce the reading of these places and actions to build confidence for speaking activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's focus on reading about places and activities provides the foundational input for students to actively speak and interact about these locations in Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction). They will use the sentences they practiced reading to form simple dialogues.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>